\function nhn-months() [function[nhn-month-raw]unique[]] :sort[function[nhn-month-ord]] +[join[, ]]

Projection functions (tiddler → value) for the §3.3 extracts and reused by §3.4/3.5/3.6.

* `nhn-serviceid` — the `TjenesteID` field.
* `nhn-url` — permalink: base URL + `#` + URI-encoded title.
* `nhn-division` — the division tag (`Divisjon …`, excluding `Domene …`).
* `nhn-servicename` — the service this tiddler is about: its service-entity tag, or its own title if it is itself a service.
* `nhn-severity` — the `Forretningsmessig endring:N` tag (business change).
* `nhn-governance-type` — the `Styring …` governance tag.
* `nhn-service-type` — the bare service-type tags: the current five (`Ekstern tjeneste`, `Intern tjeneste`, `Relatert tjeneste`, `Satsing for fart`, `Oppgaver fra HOD`), plus the casing variants and the retired `Tiltak` / `Satsning for fart`, which no tiddler carries after the September 2026 clean-up but which cost nothing to keep recognising. Since that clean-up a service carries `Ekstern tjeneste` //and// at most one of the last three, so this projection can return two values; the extract takes the first.
* `nhn-years` / `nhn-months` — //every// year or month the tiddler is tagged with, as one comma-separated cell.

(`nhn-year` / `nhn-month` live in [[$:/plugins/intertwingled-innovations/nhn/review]].)

! Single values versus all values

The group projections `nhn-year` and `nhn-month` return one value per tag, so a tiddler tagged three years appears under all three in a grouped tree — which is what grouping should do.

The CSV exporter takes only the //first// result of a projection, so those same functions would export a tiddler tagged `2024 2025 2026` as plain `2024` — a row that reads as 2024 even when the user filtered for 2026. The extracts therefore use `nhn-years` and `nhn-months`, which fold every value into one cell. Both deduplicate first: the data contains tiddlers tagged `april` //and// `April`, which normalise to the same month.
